[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108699,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pigmented spindle cell naevus, also termed Reed naevus (RN), is a benign proliferation of spindle naevomelanocytes that can show clinical and histological features mimicking a melanoma. Genomic studies have demonstrated that RN is a subtype of Spitz naevus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108700,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8770/0 Pigmented spindle cell naevus (Reed naevus)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108701,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F20.Y &amp; XH2P88 Other specific types of melanocytic naevus &amp; Pigmented spindle cell naevus of Reed</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108702,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Acceptable:</em> naevus of Reed; pigmented spindle cell Spitz naevus.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-08T17:30:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108703,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "<p>Atypical pigmented spindle cell tumour</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-08T17:30:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108704,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN most commonly involves the extremities, particularly the thigh, followed by the trunk and the head and neck {1985078; 6476194; 8463457}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108705,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN usually manifests as an acquired asymptomatic, dome-shaped, dark-brown or black macule or papule {1985078}. A hypopigmented appearance has also been described {18547345}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108706,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN preferentially affects young people (mean age: 25&nbsp;years, range: 3&ndash;66&nbsp;years) and shows a female preponderance {1985078; 6476194; 8463457}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108707,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108708,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN is caused by chromosomal structural rearrangements resulting in constitutively activated chimeric fusion proteins. Over half of the cases are the result of fusions involving <em>NTRK3</em> {29794873; 2265091}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108709,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN is a well-circumscribed, dark papule measuring &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;mm.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108710,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN is a symmetrical and well-circumscribed melanocytic neoplasm characterized by junctional nests of uniform and heavily pigmented spindle cells without atypia, limited to the epidermis or papillary dermis. Nests are vertically oriented, whorled, or concentric. In some cases the nests can extend to the papillary dermis and involve adnexa. Kamino bodies (sometimes pigmented) and pagetoid melanocytosis may be seen. Dermal melanophages are usually present {8463457; 1234372}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108711,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108712,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See the <em>Diagnostic molecular pathology</em> subsection of <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/44/373\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Spitz naevus</em> . A&nbsp;break-apart FISH probe for <em>NTRK3</em> can confirm rearrangement of <em>NTRK3</em>, the most common fusion in RN {29794873}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108713,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> junctional nests of spindle naevomelanocytes; no atypical features.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> abundant melanin in naevomelanocytes and macrophages; break-apart FISH for <em>NTRK3</em> or next-generation sequencing to verify an associated fusion {29794873}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108714,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108715,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 510,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RN is a benign lesion with a low risk of recurrence after adequate excision.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-13T11:18:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]